[F1] The global charge form is $\beta_B=\frac{1}{8\pi^2}B(F\wedge F)$, and $q_B=\int_X\beta_B$; the Hodge star and the self-dual/ASD decomposition are those fixed by the orientation and metric. [[def-qftx-instanton-number-and-asd-sector]]

[F2] The bilinear form $B$ is positive definite, and in four oriented Riemannian dimensions the Hodge star on two-forms is an orthogonal involution whose $+1$ and $-1$ eigenspaces are orthogonal. [[def-qftx-instanton-number-and-asd-sector]]

[F3] The local curvature satisfies $D_{A_i}F_i=dF_i+[A_i\wedge F_i]=0$. [[lem-qftx-principal-curvature-cartan-bianchi]]

## Proof

1.1 Write $F_i=F_i^++F_i^-$ with $*_gF_i^+=F_i^+$ and $*_gF_i^-=-F_i^-$. Let $a=\int_X B(F_i^+\wedge *_gF_i^+)$ and $b=\int_X B(F_i^-\wedge *_gF_i^-)$. Orthogonality of the two Hodge eigenspaces makes the cross terms vanish; positivity of $B$ gives $a,b\geq0$. Consequently $\int_XB(F_i\wedge *_gF_i)=a+b$, while $\int_XB(F_i\wedge F_i)=a-b$. By [F1], $S_B=(a+b)/g^2$ and $q_B=(a-b)/(8\pi^2)$. [given, F1, F2, algebra]

1.2 For nonnegative $a,b$, $a+b\geq|a-b|$, so the claimed bound follows after division by $g^2>0$. If $q_B>0$, then $a>b$ and equality is equivalent to $b=0$, which by positive definiteness is $F_i^-=0$. If $q_B<0$, equality is equivalent to $a=0$, hence $F_i^+=0$. If $q_B=0$, equality forces $a=b=0$, hence $F_i^+=F_i^-=0$ and $F_i=0$. [F1, F2, step 1.1, algebra]

