+
+## Facts & Assumptions
+
+**Given:** Full AC; a compact matrix group $G$, a smooth right principal $G$-bundle $P\to X$, a specified bundle sector, a supplied connection $\omega$, and an invariant positive-definite bilinear form $B$.
+
+[A1] Full AC means every family of nonempty sets has a choice function. [[def-axiom-of-choice]]
+
+[F1] The global group $G$, its Lie algebra, the right principal bundle, and the chosen bundle sector are part of the gauge datum. [[def-qftx-nonabelian-gauge-data]]
+
+[F2] The principal form has local potentials $A_i=s_i^*\omega$; the associated representation bundle has local connection matrix $\rho_*(A_i)$, and a unitary representation gives its Hermitian metric. [[def-qftx-principal-connection-form]], [[def-qftx-associated-covariant-derivative]]
+
+[F3] In local sections, $F_i=dA_i+\tfrac12[A_i\wedge A_i]$, $F_j=\operatorname{Ad}_{g_{ij}^{-1}}F_i$, and $D_{A_i}F_i=dF_i+[A_i\wedge F_i]=0$. [[def-qftx-lie-algebra-valued-form-bracket]], [[lem-qftx-principal-curvature-cartan-bianchi]]
+
+[F4] The polarization of an invariant quadratic polynomial on a matrix Lie algebra evaluates on curvature to a global closed form, and the corresponding Chern–Weil class is connection-independent for supplied compatible connections. [[def-invariant-polynomial-on-a-matrix-lie-algebra]], [[def-evaluation-of-an-invariant-polynomial-on-curvature]], [[def-the-chern-weil-homomorphism]], [[thm-chern-weil-homomorphism-is-independent-of-connection-and-natural]]
+
+[F5] The Chern forms are the degree components of $\det(I-\Omega/(2\pi i))$. For an $SU(2)$ connection in the defining representation, $\operatorname{tr}_2\Omega=0$. [[def-chern-pontryagin-and-euler-characteristic-forms]]
+
+[F6] With full AC, the Chern form class is the real coefficient image of the integral topological Chern class. [[thm-chern-weil-forms-represent-the-at-characteristic-classes-over-the-reals]], [[def-chern-classes-from-the-projective-bundle-relation]]
+
+[F7] A Hermitian connection satisfies $Xh(s,t)=h(\nabla_Xs,t)+h(s,\nabla_Xt)$; for an $SU(2)$ local matrix $A_i^*=-A_i$, this identity holds for the defining associated connection. [[def-complex-linear-and-compatible-bundle-connections]]
+
+[F8] The de Rham isomorphism is induced by integration on smooth singular simplices, so its evaluation on the oriented fundamental class is integration of a top-degree form. [[thm-de-rham-theorem]]
+
+## Proof
+
+1.1 On an overlap with $s_j=s_i g_{ij}$, [F3] gives $F_j=\operatorname{Ad}_{g_{ij}^{-1}}F_i$. Since $B$ is invariant, $B(F_j\wedge F_j)=B(F_i\wedge F_i)$; these restrictions therefore define the global four-form $\beta_B$. The polynomial $P_B(Z)=B(Z,Z)/(8\pi^2)$ is homogeneous, quadratic, and $G$-invariant, so its curvature evaluation [F4] is exactly $\beta_B$. Connection independence of its Chern–Weil class follows from the supplied-connection clause of [F4]. [given, F3, F4, algebra]
+
+1.2 For any two supplied connections on the same $G$-reduction, [F4] makes their Chern–Weil classes equal, so integration over the fixed orientation class gives the same $q_B$. In the $SU(2)$ defining representation, the associated connection matrix is $A_i$ because $\rho_*$ is the identity inclusion, and its curvature matrix is $\Omega_i=F_i$ by [F2, F3]. Put $C=i\Omega_i/(2\pi)=-\Omega_i/(2\pi i)$. Its entries have even form degree and commute, so the degree-four coefficient of $\det(I+C)$ is $\det C=\tfrac12((\operatorname{tr}_2C)^2-\operatorname{tr}_2(C\wedge C))$. By [F5], $\operatorname{tr}_2C=0$, and $C\wedge C=-\Omega_i\wedge\Omega_i/(4\pi^2)$; hence $c_2(\nabla)=\operatorname{tr}_2(\Omega_i\wedge\Omega_i)/(8\pi^2)$ and $\beta_B=-c_2(\nabla)$ because $B=-\operatorname{tr}_2$. The associated connection is Hermitian by [F7], so [F6] sends this de Rham class to the negative realification of $c_2(E)\in H^4(X;\mathbb Z)$. Evaluating by [F8] on the orientation class gives $q_B=-\langle c_2(E),[X]\rangle\in\mathbb Z$. Full AC is used only through the stated characteristic-class comparison and integral-class construction; the local overlap calculation in step 1.1 uses no choice. [A1, F2, F3, F4, F5, F6, F7, F8, algebra]
+
+2.1 The oriented Riemannian metric defines the Hodge operator on two-forms and $*_g^2=I$ in dimension four. The curvature transforms by the adjoint action on overlaps, while $*_g$ acts only on the form arguments, so each $F_i^\pm=(F_i\pm *_gF_i)/2$ transforms by the same adjoint action. Thus $F^\pm$ are global adjoint-valued two-forms and the stated self-dual and ASD conditions do not depend on the local sections. [F3, given, algebra] ∎
