-+## Remarks
-+
-+The square records the change of local connection form across the overlap and its induced curvature change. Step 2.1 verifies the top arrow, and step 3.1 verifies the curvature calculation; the proof does not rely on the diagram.
-+
-+```tikzcd
-+\Omega^1(U_N\cap U_S,\mathfrak{su}(2)) \arrow[r,"A\mapsto\operatorname{Ad}_uA+u\,d(u^{-1})"] \arrow[d,"A\mapsto dA+\tfrac12[A\wedge A]"'] & \Omega^1(U_N\cap U_S,\mathfrak{su}(2)) \arrow[d,"A\mapsto dA+\tfrac12[A\wedge A]"] \\
-+\Omega^2(U_N\cap U_S,\mathfrak{su}(2)) \arrow[r,"F\mapsto\operatorname{Ad}_uF"'] & \Omega^2(U_N\cap U_S,\mathfrak{su}(2))
-+```
+Assume full Axiom of Choice. Let $S^4=\{z\in\mathbb R^5:|z|=1\}$ have its unit round metric $g_X$. Denote by $p_\infty$ the point omitted by the north stereographic chart and by $p_0$ the point with north-chart coordinates $x=0$. Orient $S^4$ so that north-chart coordinates $x=(x^1,x^2,x^3,x^4)$ satisfy $dx^1\wedge dx^2\wedge dx^3\wedge dx^4>0$. Set $r^2=\sum_{\mu=1}^4(x^\mu)^2$. The metric in the north chart is $4(1+r^2)^{-2}\sum_{\mu=1}^4(dx^\mu)^2$. Let $U_N=S^4\setminus\{p_\infty\}$ carry coordinate $x$, and $U_S=S^4\setminus\{p_0\}$ carry coordinate $y=x/r^2$; the metric in the south chart is $4(1+|y|^2)^{-2}\sum_{\mu=1}^4(dy^\mu)^2$.
+
+Define $SU(2)=\{U\in M_2(\mathbb C):U^*U=I,\det U=1\}$. Write $\epsilon_{123}=1$ and let
+
+$$ \sigma_1=\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\1&0\end{pmatrix},\qquad \sigma_2=\begin{pmatrix}0&-i\\i&0\end{pmatrix},\qquad \sigma_3=\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\0&-1\end{pmatrix},\qquad T_a=-\frac{i}{2}\sigma_a. $$
+
+The $T_a$ are traceless anti-Hermitian generators. Set $B(X,Y)=-\operatorname{tr}_2(XY)$. Define antisymmetric symbols by $\eta_{aij}=\epsilon_{aij}$, $\eta_{ai4}=\delta_{ai}$, $\bar\eta_{aij}=-\epsilon_{aij}$, and $\bar\eta_{ai4}=\delta_{ai}$ for spatial indices $i,j\in\{1,2,3\}$, where $\delta_{ai}$ is the Kronecker delta; extend them antisymmetrically in the last two indices. For a scale parameter $\rho>0$, define $u(x)=(x^4I-i x^j\sigma_j)/r\in SU(2)$ on $U_N\cap U_S$. Glue the trivial right $SU(2)$-bundles on $U_N$ and $U_S$ by the local-section transition $s_S=s_Nu^{-1}$, and define their local connection potentials by
+
+$$ A_N=\frac{2\eta_{a\mu\nu}x^\nu}{r^2+\rho^2}T_a\,dx^\mu,\qquad A_S=-\frac{2\rho^2\bar\eta_{a\mu\nu}y^\nu}{1+\rho^2|y|^2}T_a\,dy^\mu. $$
+
+On the overlap these obey $A_S=\operatorname{Ad}_u A_N+u\,d(u^{-1})$, so they define a smooth principal connection on the glued bundle. Let $F$ be its global curvature, with chart representatives $F_N$ and $F_S$. In the north chart its curvature, written $F_N=\tfrac12F^N_{\mu\nu}dx^\mu\wedge dx^\nu$, has components
+
+$$ F^N_{\mu\nu}=-\frac{4\rho^2}{(r^2+\rho^2)^2}\eta_{a\mu\nu}T_a. $$
+
+Let $*_{g_X}$ be the Hodge star characterized on two-forms by $\alpha\wedge *_{g_X}\beta=\langle\alpha,\beta\rangle_{g_X}\,d\operatorname{vol}_X$, acting componentwise on the Lie-algebra coefficients. This curvature is self-dual, meaning $F_N=*_{g_X}F_N$. With $B$ as fixed above, define the charge by $q_B=(8\pi^2)^{-1}\int_{S^4}B(F\wedge F)$ and, for any real $g>0$, define the action by $S_B(g)=g^{-2}\int_{S^4}B(F\wedge *_{g_X}F)$. Then $q_B=1$, $S_B(g)=8\pi^2/g^2<\infty$, and the action saturates the self-dual bound. This is one explicit Belavin–Polyakov–Schwartz–Tyupkin (BPST) charge-one connection; no uniqueness or moduli classification is asserted.
+
+## Example references
+
+[Belavin–Polyakov–Schwartz–Tyupkin, printed pp. 85–87](https://strings.itp.ac.ru/Papers/belavin1975.pdf)
+
+[Tong, Lecture 1 §§1.1–1.1.2](https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/tasi/instanton.pdf)
+
+[[def-qftx-instanton-number-and-asd-sector|Trace form, charge, and Chern–Weil sign convention]]
+
+[[thm-qftx-yang-mills-selfdual-action-bound|Self-dual action bound and Bianchi consequence]]
+
+[[lem-qftx-principal-curvature-cartan-bianchi|Local curvature and section-transition convention]]
+
+[[ex-unitary-and-special-unitary-lie-groups|SU(2) matrix Lie group and Lie algebra]]
+
+[[thm-heine-borel-rn|Closed-and-bounded compactness in the matrix coordinate space]]
+
+## Facts & Assumptions
+
+**Given:** Full AC; the round sphere charts and orientation; the Pauli matrices, symbols, scale $\rho>0$, and trace form $B$ in the Example statement.
+
+[A1] Full AC means every family of nonempty sets has a choice function. [[def-axiom-of-choice]]
+
+[F1] $SU(2)$ is a real matrix Lie group with Lie algebra the traceless skew-Hermitian matrices and commutator bracket. [[ex-unitary-and-special-unitary-lie-groups]]
+
+[F2] A closed and bounded subset of Euclidean $\mathbb R^8$ is compact. [[thm-heine-borel-rn]]
+
+[F3] For a right principal bundle, the local-section change $s_S=s_Ng$ gives $A_S=\operatorname{Ad}_{g^{-1}}A_N+g^{-1}dg$ and $F_S=\operatorname{Ad}_{g^{-1}}F_N$; the local curvature is $dA+\tfrac12[A\wedge A]$. [[def-qftx-principal-connection-form]], [[lem-qftx-principal-curvature-cartan-bianchi]]
+
+[F4] The graded bracket convention gives $\tfrac12[A\wedge A](\partial_\mu,\partial_\nu)=[A_\mu,A_\nu]$. [[def-qftx-lie-algebra-valued-form-bracket]]
+
+[F5] The charge is $q_B=(8\pi^2)^{-1}\int B(F\wedge F)$ and the action is $S_B=g^{-2}\int B(F\wedge *_{g_X}F)$; a self-dual connection with positive charge saturates $S_B\geq8\pi^2|q_B|/g^2$. [[def-qftx-instanton-number-and-asd-sector]], [[thm-qftx-yang-mills-selfdual-action-bound]]
+
+[F6] Gauge data specify the actual global group, right principal bundle, representation family, and admitted bundle sector; the datum itself does not choose a connection. [[def-qftx-nonabelian-gauge-data]]
+
+[F7] Two trivial right principal bundles glued on an open overlap by a smooth group-valued transition map form a smooth right principal bundle, and the local sections obey the declared transition. [[lem-qftx-two-chart-principal-bundle-gluing]]
+
+
+## Verification
+
+1.1 The matrix $x^4I-i x^j\sigma_j$ has adjoint product $r^2I$ and determinant $r^2$, so $u(x)$ is unitary with determinant one wherever $r>0$. The equations $U^*U=I$ and $\det U=1$ make $SU(2)$ closed in the eight-dimensional real matrix space, and its entries have modulus at most one, so it is bounded; [F2] gives compactness. The matrices $T_a$ are traceless and skew-Hermitian by their entries; direct multiplication gives $[T_a,T_b]=\epsilon_{abc}T_c$ and $B(T_a,T_b)=\tfrac12\delta_{ab}$. Also $\sigma_1\sigma_2\ne\sigma_2\sigma_1$, so $SU(2)$ is non-Abelian. Together with [F1], this verifies the group and trace hypotheses of the definition. [given, F1, F2, algebra]
+
+2.1 By [F7], the quotient with $\tau=u^{-1}$ is a smooth right principal $SU(2)$-bundle with sections satisfying $s_S=s_Nu^{-1}$. Full AC [A1] supplies the countable-choice hypothesis of the gauge-data interface [F6], which specifies the actual global form, singleton defining-representation family, and bundle sector selected in the Example statement. Matrix multiplication using $\sigma_i\sigma_j=\delta_{ij}I+i\epsilon_{ijk}\sigma_k$ gives $u^{-1}du=\frac{2\eta_{a\mu\nu}x^\nu}{r^2}T_a\,dx^\mu$ and $du\,u^{-1}=\frac{2\bar\eta_{a\mu\nu}x^\nu}{r^2}T_a\,dx^\mu$. Thus $A_N=\frac{r^2}{r^2+\rho^2}u^{-1}du$. With transition $g=u^{-1}$, [F3] gives $\operatorname{Ad}_u A_N+u\,d(u^{-1})=-\frac{\rho^2}{r^2+\rho^2}du\,u^{-1}=-\frac{2\rho^2\bar\eta_{a\mu\nu}x^\nu}{r^2(r^2+\rho^2)}T_a\,dx^\mu$. Substituting $y=x/r^2$ and $dx^\mu=(\delta_{\mu\lambda}/|y|^2-2y^\mu y_\lambda/|y|^4)dy^\lambda$, the term containing $y^\mu y^\nu\bar\eta_{a\mu\nu}$ vanishes by antisymmetry, leaving exactly the displayed $A_S$. The $A_N$ coefficients are smooth at $x=0$ because $\rho>0$, and $A_S$ is smooth at $y=0$ because its denominator is positive and its numerator is linear in $y$. Hence the two forms define a smooth global connection with the declared patch-at-infinity transition. [given, A1, F3, F6, F7, step 1.1, algebra]
+
+3.1 Differentiating $A^a_{N,\nu}=2\eta_{a\nu\lambda}x^\lambda/(r^2+\rho^2)$ and using [F4] gives
+$$ F^a_{N,\mu\nu}=-\frac{4\eta_{a\mu\nu}}{r^2+\rho^2}+\frac{4}{(r^2+\rho^2)^2}\left(-\eta_{a\nu\lambda}x^\lambda x_\mu+\eta_{a\mu\lambda}x^\lambda x_\nu+\epsilon_{bca}\eta_{b\mu\lambda}\eta_{c\nu\kappa}x^\lambda x^\kappa\right). $$
+For $\mu=i,\nu=4$, substituting the defining symbols and $\sum_b\epsilon_{bka}\epsilon_{bij}=\delta_{ki}\delta_{aj}-\delta_{kj}\delta_{ai}$ reduces the parenthesis to $r^2\delta_{ai}$. For $\mu=i,\nu=j$, the terms with one index $4$ cancel in pairs; the spatial terms use $\sum_b\epsilon_{bka}\epsilon_{bi\ell}=\delta_{ki}\delta_{a\ell}-\delta_{k\ell}\delta_{ai}$ and $\epsilon_{ijk}x_a x_k-\epsilon_{ajk}x_kx_i+\epsilon_{aik}x_kx_j=\epsilon_{aij}\sum_{k=1}^3x_k^2$, while the two-index-$4$ term contributes $\epsilon_{aij}(x^4)^2$. Thus the parenthesis is $r^2\eta_{a\mu\nu}$ for all independent index pairs, and $F^a_{N,\mu\nu}=-4\rho^2\eta_{a\mu\nu}/(r^2+\rho^2)^2$. The explicit two-forms are $\eta_1=dx^{14}+dx^{23}$, $\eta_2=dx^{24}+dx^{31}$, and $\eta_3=dx^{34}+dx^{12}$; each is self-dual in the chosen orientation. The round metric is conformal to the Euclidean metric, and in four dimensions the Hodge star on two-forms is unchanged by conformal rescaling. Therefore $F_N=*_{g_X}F_N$. [given, F3, F4, step 2.1, algebra]
+
+4.1 The trace form gives $B(T_a,T_b)=\tfrac12\delta_{ab}$ and the defining symbols satisfy $\sum_{a,\mu,\nu}\eta_{a\mu\nu}^2=12$. Since $F_N=\tfrac12F_{N,\mu\nu}dx^\mu\wedge dx^\nu$, the local action density is $B(F_N\wedge *_{g_X}F_N)=48\rho^4(r^2+\rho^2)^{-4}d^4x$. The radial substitution $t=r^2/\rho^2$ gives $\int_{\mathbb R^4}\rho^4(r^2+\rho^2)^{-4}d^4x=2\pi^2\cdot\tfrac12\int_0^\infty t(1+t)^{-4}dt=\pi^2/6$, hence $\int_{S^4}B(F\wedge *_{g_X}F)=8\pi^2$; the omitted stereographic point has measure zero and the patched curvature is smooth. Self-duality gives $B(F\wedge F)=B(F\wedge *_{g_X}F)$, so $q_B=1$. The action is $S_B=8\pi^2/g^2$, finite and equal to the bound in [F5]. The definition and bound interfaces invoked in [F5] both assume full AC [A1]. [given, A1, F5, step 3.1, algebra] ∎
+
+## Remarks
+The square records the change of local connection form across the overlap and its induced curvature change. Step 2.1 verifies the top arrow, and step 3.1 verifies the curvature calculation; the proof does not rely on the diagram.
+```tikzcd
+\Omega^1(U_N\cap U_S,\mathfrak{su}(2)) \arrow[r,"A\mapsto\operatorname{Ad}_uA+u\,d(u^{-1})"] \arrow[d,"A\mapsto dA+\tfrac12[A\wedge A]"'] & \Omega^1(U_N\cap U_S,\mathfrak{su}(2)) \arrow[d,"A\mapsto dA+\tfrac12[A\wedge A]"] \\
+\Omega^2(U_N\cap U_S,\mathfrak{su}(2)) \arrow[r,"F\mapsto\operatorname{Ad}_uF"'] & \Omega^2(U_N\cap U_S,\mathfrak{su}(2))
