+---
+
+## Statement
+
+Let $V$ be a four-dimensional real inner-product space with an orientation, and let $W$ be a finite-dimensional real inner-product space. Define the Hodge operator $*_V$ on $\Lambda^2V^*$ by $\alpha\wedge *_V\beta=\langle\alpha,\beta\rangle_V\,\mathrm{vol}_V$, and extend it $W$-linearly to $\Lambda^2V^*\otimes W$. Give this tensor product the inner product induced by those on $\Lambda^2V^*$ and $W$. Then $*_V^2=I$, $*_V$ is an isometry, and its $+1$ and $-1$ eigenspaces are orthogonal. Every $\Phi\in\Lambda^2V^*\otimes W$ has the unique decomposition
+
+$$
+\Phi=\Phi^++\Phi^-,\qquad \Phi^\pm:=\frac{\Phi\pm *_V\Phi}{2},\qquad *_V\Phi^\pm=\pm\Phi^\pm.
+$$
+
+If the inner product on $V$ is rescaled to $\widehat g=\lambda^2g$ for any $\lambda>0$, then the Hodge operator on two-forms is unchanged: $*_{\widehat g}=*_g$.
+
+## Statement references
+
+[Tong, Lecture 1 §1.1.1, equations (1.6)–(1.8)](https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/tasi/instanton.pdf)
+
+## Facts & Assumptions
+
+**Given:** The oriented four-dimensional inner-product space $V$, the finite-dimensional inner-product space $W$, and the Hodge operator and induced tensor-product inner product specified in the Statement.
+
+[A1] The oriented space $V$ has a positively oriented orthonormal coframe, and $W$ has an orthonormal basis; these are finite-dimensional inner-product spaces.
+
+## Proof
+
+1.1 Choose a positively oriented orthonormal coframe $e^1,e^2,e^3,e^4$ of $V^*$. The defining wedge identity gives $*_V e^{12}=e^{34}$, $*_V e^{13}=-e^{24}$, $*_V e^{14}=e^{23}$, $*_V e^{23}=e^{14}$, $*_V e^{24}=-e^{13}$, and $*_V e^{34}=e^{12}$. Thus $*_V$ permutes the orthonormal basis of $\Lambda^2V^*$ with signs, so it is an isometry; applying it twice to each of the six basis vectors gives $*_V^2=I$. The same conclusions hold after tensoring each basis vector with any member of an orthonormal basis of $W$. [given, A1, algebra]
+
+2.1 For $\Phi^+=\tfrac12(\Phi+*_V\Phi)$ and $\Phi^-=\tfrac12(\Phi-*_V\Phi)$, the identity $*_V^2=I$ from step 1.1 gives $*_V\Phi^+=\Phi^+$, $*_V\Phi^-=-\Phi^-$, and $\Phi=\Phi^++\Phi^-$. If $*_V\alpha=\alpha$ and $*_V\beta=-\beta$, the isometry property from step 1.1 gives $\langle\alpha,\beta\rangle=\langle *_V\alpha,*_V\beta\rangle=-\langle\alpha,\beta\rangle$, hence $\langle\alpha,\beta\rangle=0$. The decomposition is unique because the intersection of the two eigenspaces is zero. [given, A1, step 1.1, algebra]
+
+3.1 Under $\widehat g=\lambda^2g$, the induced inner product on $\Lambda^2V^*$ scales by $\lambda^{-4}$ because each covector factor scales by $\lambda^{-2}$, while the oriented volume form scales by $\lambda^4$. Hence for all $\alpha,\beta\in\Lambda^2V^*$, $\langle\alpha,\beta\rangle_{\widehat g}\,\mathrm{vol}_{\widehat g}=\langle\alpha,\beta\rangle_g\,\mathrm{vol}_g$. The defining wedge identity therefore gives $\alpha\wedge *_{\widehat g}\beta=\alpha\wedge *_g\beta$ for every $\alpha$; nondegeneracy of the wedge pairing between two-forms in dimension four implies $*_{\widehat g}\beta=*_g\beta$. The identity extends $W$-linearly. [given, A1, algebra] ∎
