## Verification

1.1 The matrix $x^4I-i x^j\sigma_j$ has adjoint product $r^2I$ and determinant $r^2$, so $u(x)$ is unitary with determinant one wherever $r>0$. The equations $U^*U=I$ and $\det U=1$ make $SU(2)$ closed in the eight-dimensional real matrix space, and its entries have modulus at most one, so it is bounded; [F2] gives compactness. The matrices $T_a$ are traceless and skew-Hermitian by their entries; direct multiplication gives $[T_a,T_b]=\epsilon_{abc}T_c$ and $B(T_a,T_b)=\tfrac12\delta_{ab}$. Also $\sigma_1\sigma_2\ne\sigma_2\sigma_1$, so $SU(2)$ is non-Abelian. Together with [F1], this verifies the group and trace hypotheses of the definition. [given, F1, F2, algebra]

2.1 By [F7], the quotient with $\tau=u^{-1}$ is a smooth right principal $SU(2)$-bundle with sections satisfying $s_S=s_Nu^{-1}$. Full AC [A1] supplies the countable-choice hypothesis of the gauge-data interface [F6], which specifies the actual global form, singleton defining-representation family, and bundle sector selected in the Example statement. Matrix multiplication using $\sigma_i\sigma_j=\delta_{ij}I+i\epsilon_{ijk}\sigma_k$ gives $u^{-1}du=\frac{2\eta_{a\mu\nu}x^\nu}{r^2}T_a\,dx^\mu$ and $du\,u^{-1}=\frac{2\bar\eta_{a\mu\nu}x^\nu}{r^2}T_a\,dx^\mu$. Thus $A_N=\frac{r^2}{r^2+\rho^2}u^{-1}du$. With transition $g=u^{-1}$, [F3] gives $\operatorname{Ad}_u A_N+u\,d(u^{-1})=-\frac{\rho^2}{r^2+\rho^2}du\,u^{-1}=-\frac{2\rho^2\bar\eta_{a\mu\nu}x^\nu}{r^2(r^2+\rho^2)}T_a\,dx^\mu$. Substituting $y=x/r^2$ and $dx^\mu=(\delta_{\mu\lambda}/|y|^2-2y^\mu y_\lambda/|y|^4)dy^\lambda$, the term containing $y^\mu y^\nu\bar\eta_{a\mu\nu}$ vanishes by antisymmetry, leaving exactly the displayed $A_S$. The $A_N$ coefficients are smooth at $x=0$ because $\rho>0$, and $A_S$ is smooth at $y=0$ because its denominator is positive and its numerator is linear in $y$. Hence the two forms define a smooth global connection with the declared patch-at-infinity transition. [given, A1, F3, F6, F7, step 1.1, algebra]

3.1 Differentiating $A^a_{N,\nu}=2\eta_{a\nu\lambda}x^\lambda/(r^2+\rho^2)$ and using [F4] gives $F^a_{N,\mu\nu}=-4\eta_{a\mu\nu}/D+4Q_{a\mu\nu}/D^2$, where $D=r^2+\rho^2$ and $Q_{a\mu\nu}=-\eta_{a\nu\lambda}x^\lambda x_\mu+\eta_{a\mu\lambda}x^\lambda x_\nu+\epsilon_{bca}\eta_{b\mu\lambda}\eta_{c\nu\kappa}x^\lambda x^\kappa$. Put $t=x^4$ and $|\vec x|^2=\sum_{k=1}^3(x^k)^2$. For $\mu=i,\nu=4$, expanding the symbols gives $Q_{ai4}=x_ax_i+t(\epsilon_{aik}x_k+\delta_{ai}t)-\epsilon_{bka}(\epsilon_{bil}x_l+\delta_{bi}t)x_k$. The contraction $\sum_b\epsilon_{bka}\epsilon_{bil}=\delta_{ki}\delta_{al}-\delta_{kl}\delta_{ai}$ makes the $x_ax_i$ terms cancel, and $\epsilon_{ika}=\epsilon_{aik}$ cancels the terms linear in $t$; hence $Q_{ai4}=(|\vec x|^2+t^2)\delta_{ai}=r^2\eta_{ai4}$. For distinct spatial $i,j$, expansion gives the derivative terms $-\epsilon_{ajl}x_lx_i-\delta_{aj}tx_i+\epsilon_{ail}x_lx_j+\delta_{ai}tx_j$. The terms in the bracket product linear in $t$ equal $t(-\delta_{ai}x_j+\delta_{aj}x_i)$, so they cancel those derivative terms. Contracting the two spatial epsilon factors by $\sum_b\epsilon_{bca}\epsilon_{bil}=\delta_{ci}\delta_{al}-\delta_{cl}\delta_{ai}$ leaves $R_{aij}=\epsilon_{ijm}x_ax_m-\epsilon_{ajl}x_lx_i+\epsilon_{ail}x_lx_j$, and the two-index-$4$ term is $t^2\epsilon_{aij}$. Let $k$ be the remaining spatial index and $\varepsilon=\epsilon_{ijk}$. If $a=i$, the first two terms of $R$ are $\varepsilon x_ix_k-\varepsilon x_kx_i=0$; if $a=j$, they are $\varepsilon x_jx_k-\varepsilon x_kx_j=0$; if $a=k$, all three terms sum to $\varepsilon(x_i^2+x_j^2+x_k^2)$. Thus $R_{aij}=\epsilon_{aij}|\vec x|^2$ and $Q_{aij}=r^2\eta_{aij}$. These are all independent antisymmetric index pairs, so $F^a_{N,\mu\nu}=-4\rho^2\eta_{a\mu\nu}/(r^2+\rho^2)^2$. The two-forms $\eta_1=dx^{14}+dx^{23}$, $\eta_2=dx^{24}+dx^{31}$, and $\eta_3=dx^{34}+dx^{12}$ are self-dual in the chosen Euclidean orientation because the star interchanges the two displayed summands in each. The round metric is conformal to the Euclidean metric, and [F8] gives the same Hodge star on two-forms; therefore $F_N=*_{g_X}F_N$. [given, F4, F8, step 2.1, algebra]

4.1 The trace form gives $B(T_a,T_b)=\tfrac12\delta_{ab}$ and the defining symbols satisfy $\sum_{a,\mu,\nu}\eta_{a\mu\nu}^2=12$. Since $F_N=\tfrac12F_{N,\mu\nu}dx^\mu\wedge dx^\nu$, the local action density is $B(F_N\wedge *_{g_X}F_N)=48\rho^4(r^2+\rho^2)^{-4}d^4x$. The radial substitution $t=r^2/\rho^2$ gives $\int_{\mathbb R^4}\rho^4(r^2+\rho^2)^{-4}d^4x=2\pi^2\cdot\tfrac12\int_0^\infty t(1+t)^{-4}dt=\pi^2/6$: with $v=1+t$, $\int_0^\infty t(1+t)^{-4}dt=\int_1^\infty(v^{-3}-v^{-4})dv=[-1/(2v^2)+1/(3v^3)]_1^\infty=1/2-1/3=1/6$. hence $\int_{S^4}B(F\wedge *_{g_X}F)=8\pi^2$; the omitted stereographic point has measure zero and the patched curvature is smooth. Self-duality gives $B(F\wedge F)=B(F\wedge *_{g_X}F)$, so $q_B=1$. The action is $S_B=8\pi^2/g^2$, finite and equal to the bound in [F5]. The definition and bound interfaces invoked in [F5] both assume full AC [A1]. [given, A1, F5, F8, step 1.1, step 3.1, algebra] ∎

