 ## Example
 
-Assume full Axiom of Choice. Let $S^4$ be the unit round four-sphere, oriented so that the north stereographic coordinates $x=(x^1,x^2,x^3,x^4)$ satisfy $dx^1\wedge dx^2\wedge dx^3\wedge dx^4>0$, with round metric $4(1+r^2)^{-2}\sum_{mu=1}^4(dx^mu)^2$ and $r^2=\sum_{mu=1}^4(x^mu)^2$. Let $U_N=S^4\setminus\{\infty\}$ carry the coordinate $x$, and let $U_S=S^4\setminus\{0\}$ carry the coordinate $y=x/r^2$; the round metric on $U_S$ is $4(1+|y|^2)^{-2}\sum_{mu=1}^4(dy^mu)^2$. Write $\epsilon_{123}=1$ and let
-+
-+$$
-+\sigma_1=\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\1&0\end{pmatrix},\qquad
-+\sigma_2=\begin{pmatrix}0&-i\\i&0\end{pmatrix},\qquad
-+\sigma_3=\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\0&-1\end{pmatrix},\qquad
-+T_a=-\frac{i}{2}\sigma_a\in\mathfrak{su}(2).
-+$$
-+
-+Set $B(X,Y)=-\operatorname{tr}_2(XY)$. Define antisymmetric symbols by $\eta_{aij}=\epsilon_{aij}$, $\eta_{ai4}=\delta_{ai}$, $\bar\eta_{aij}=-\epsilon_{aij}$, and $\bar\eta_{ai4}=\delta_{ai}$ for spatial indices $i,j\in\{1,2,3\}$, and extend antisymmetrically in the last two indices. For a scale parameter $\rho>0$, define $u(x)=(x^4I-i x^j\sigma_j)/r\in SU(2)$ on $U_N\cap U_S$. Glue the trivial right $SU(2)$-bundles on $U_N$ and $U_S$ by the local-section transition $s_S=s_Nu^{-1}$, and give them the potentials
-+
-+$$
-+A_N=\frac{2\eta_{a\mu\nu}x^\nu}{r^2+\rho^2}T_a\,dx^\mu,\qquad
-+A_S=-\frac{2\rho^2\bar\eta_{a\mu\nu}y^\nu}{1+\rho^2|y|^2}T_a\,dy^\mu.
-+$$
-+
-+On the overlap these obey $A_S=\operatorname{Ad}_u A_N+u\,d(u^{-1})$, so they define a smooth principal connection on the glued bundle. In the north chart its curvature components are
-+
-+$$
-+F^N_{\mu\nu}=-\frac{4\rho^2}{(r^2+\rho^2)^2}\eta_{a\mu\nu}T_a,
-+$$
-+
-+and this curvature is self-dual for the round metric. It has $q_B=1$, finite Yang–Mills action $S_B=8\pi^2/g^2$ for every $g>0$, and saturates the stated action bound. This is one explicit Belavin–Polyakov–Schwartz–Tyupkin (BPST) charge-one connection; no uniqueness or moduli classification is asserted.
